"62_winui_door_tests.mst — WinUiDoorTests, the headless half of WG2's
 acceptance gate (docs/sprints/tests_wg2.md, 'In-language tests').
 Exercises the guest side of the WndProc door in world/91_winui_shell.mst:
 the dispatcher's two-sided allowlist, its LRESULT contract, and the fact
 that a handler really runs in the real VM.

 WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT HERE: anything that needs a WINDOW, a message
 PUMP, or a nested Win32 SEND. Every one of D4's three failure modes — a
 raising handler, a faulting handler, a re-entered door — is a claim about
 what happens when Windows calls the trampoline, and Windows will not call
 a trampoline in a `cargo test` worker thread with no window and no loop.
 Those live in `just gate-wg2`, driven through MACVM_WINUI_CTL against one
 process with one thread and one real window, and in the door's own Rust
 unit tests (src/runtime/win_wndproc.rs), which drive a real guest fault and
 a real ACCESS_VIOLATION through the real trampoline. Drawing that line is
 the same decision 61_winui_shell_tests.mst made about windows, for the same
 reason (WG0's Δ 11).

 WHAT REMAINS, and it is the half worth having in-language: the dispatcher
 is ORDINARY SMALLTALK. `window:message:wParam:lParam:` can be SENT — no
 window, no HWND, no Win32 at all for the four messages that only record —
 so its contract is testable as a method rather than as a system.

 THE SAME TWO SKIP LEVELS as 61, both announced:
   * NOT WINDOWS — the runner never calls us (99_run_all.mst's guard).
   * NO DATABASE — the dispatcher asks winkb for every message number it
     compares against, so without windows_api.db it cannot run at all. The
     one test that needs neither winkb nor Win32 — the address channel,
     which is a primitive — still runs.

 LOADING: `WinShell` and `WinApi` live in the conditional winui layer, so
 world/tests/tests.list names ../90 and ../91 above this file. An
 unresolved capitalised name in a method body is a COMPILE error in this
 dialect, so a layered world's test file cannot guard itself at run time
 (WG0's Δ 6, inherited, not re-litigated)."

TestCase subclass: WinUiDoorTests [

    runAll [
        "The address channel is a primitive and needs nothing else — not
         winkb, not Win32, not a window — so it runs whenever this file
         runs at all. It is also the one thing WG2 could not have done
         without new Rust (WG0's Δ 8), which makes it the first thing to
         check."
        self runTest: #testDoorAddressIsPublished do: [ self testDoorAddressIsPublished ].

        WinApi winkbAvailable ifFalse: [
            Transcript showCr:
                '  SKIP WinUiDoorTests (4 of 5) — windows_api.db absent on this machine.'.
            Transcript showCr:
                '       The dispatcher compares every message against a winkb-queried'.
            Transcript showCr:
                '       number (it transcribes none), so testUnhandledMessageAnswersDefault,'.
            Transcript showCr:
                '       testHandlerReturnsLResult, testHandlerStateSurvives and'.
            Transcript showCr:
                '       testAllowlistMatchesWinkb cannot run. The database is a'.
            Transcript showCr:
                '       machine-local artifact, not a build input; nothing here is broken.'.
            ^self ].

        self runTest: #testUnhandledMessageAnswersDefault
             do: [ self testUnhandledMessageAnswersDefault ].
        self runTest: #testHandlerReturnsLResult do: [ self testHandlerReturnsLResult ].
        self runTest: #testHandlerStateSurvives do: [ self testHandlerStateSurvives ].
        self runTest: #testAllowlistMatchesWinkb do: [ self testAllowlistMatchesWinkb ].
    ]

    "── D2: the address channel ──────────────────────────────────────────"

    testDoorAddressIsPublished [
        "WG0's Δ 8 answered. Nothing in this port published a RUST function's
         address to Smalltalk — the FFI resolves by NAME and GetProcAddress
         only reaches DLL exports — so without primitive 272 a Smalltalk-built
         WNDCLASSW could name DefWindowProcW and nothing else.

         Stable, because an address that changed between two asks would mean
         somebody made this mint a trampoline per call; and a SmallInteger,
         because an address the guest cannot hold is not a channel. It must
         also be DIFFERENT from DefWindowProcW's — a door that turned out to
         be the system default would pass every behavioural test in this file
         while doing nothing at all."
        | a b |
        a := WinApi primWndProcAddress.
        self assert: a notNil.
        self deny: a = 0.
        b := WinApi primWndProcAddress.
        self assert: b equals: a.
        self assert: WinApi wndProcAddress equals: a.
        self assert: (a isKindOf: Integer).
        "The comparison against DefWindowProcW is inside a database guard and
         nothing else in this test is, which is worth a sentence: learning the
         SYSTEM handler's address goes through GetProcAddress, whose symbol
         name lives in the arena, whose VirtualAlloc needs MEM_COMMIT — a
         winkb constant. So the one assertion that needs the database is the
         one about the address this port could already reach, and the new
         channel needs nothing at all. That asymmetry is the point of D2."
        WinApi winkbAvailable ifTrue: [
            self deny: a = WinApi defWindowProcAddress ]
    ]

    "── D1: the allowlist, from the guest's side ─────────────────────────"

    testUnhandledMessageAnswersDefault [
        "The two-sided allowlist agreeing in the SAFE direction. Rust asks
         only for six messages; this class answers #defwindowproc for
         anything it does not itself handle. The halves may therefore
         disagree — a message added to one list before the other — and the
         result is always 'behaves as it did in WG1', never 'a window that
         stops responding'.

         WM_MOUSEMOVE and WM_NCHITTEST are the two the Rust half will never
         forward, so this is belt AND braces: they are asserted here because
         a future sprint that adds one to the Rust allowlist without adding a
         handler must still get a working window."
        self assert: (WinShell window: 0 message: 16r0200 wParam: 0 lParam: 0)
             equals: #defwindowproc.
        self assert: (WinShell window: 0 message: 16r0084 wParam: 0 lParam: 0)
             equals: #defwindowproc.
        self assert: (WinShell window: 0 message: (WinApi constant: 'WM_PAINT') wParam: 0 lParam: 0)
             equals: #defwindowproc.
        "And the three ALLOWLISTED messages whose meaning is still WG3/WG5's
         answer the same way — recorded, then defaulted. Swallowing a key
         here would break Alt-F4 and every accelerator Windows handles free."
        self assert: (WinShell window: 0 message: (WinApi constant: 'WM_KEYDOWN')
                               wParam: 65 lParam: 0)
             equals: #defwindowproc.
        self assert: (WinShell window: 0 message: (WinApi constant: 'WM_CHAR')
                               wParam: 65 lParam: 0)
             equals: #defwindowproc.
        self assert: (WinShell window: 0 message: (WinApi constant: 'WM_COMMAND')
                               wParam: 7 lParam: 0)
             equals: #defwindowproc.
        "...but they were RECORDED on the way through, which is what makes
         them handled-but-defaulted rather than ignored."
        self assert: WinShell lastKey equals: 65.
        self assert: WinShell lastCommand equals: 7
    ]

    testHandlerReturnsLResult [
        "A handled message answers an Integer, which is the door's whole
         return contract: Rust reads a SmallInteger as the LRESULT and treats
         EVERY other answer — a Symbol, nil, a String, a raise — as
         'DefWindowProcW's'. So the type is the contract, and 0 for a
         processed WM_SIZE is what Windows documents."
        | r |
        WinShell resetDoorCounters.
        r := WinShell window: 0 message: (WinApi constant: 'WM_SIZE')
                      wParam: 0 lParam: 0.
        self assert: (r isKindOf: Integer).
        self assert: r equals: 0.
        "WM_CLOSE is the deliberate exception, and the reason is structural
         rather than stylistic: answering #defwindowproc puts the
         DestroyWindow that follows on the far side of the depth guard, so
         the WM_DESTROY it provokes is a fresh top-level entry and reaches
         Smalltalk. Calling DestroyWindow from inside this method would nest
         it, the guard would decline it, and PostQuitMessage would never run."
        self assert: (WinShell window: 0 message: (WinApi constant: 'WM_CLOSE')
                               wParam: 0 lParam: 0)
             equals: #defwindowproc.
        self assert: WinShell closeCount equals: 1
    ]

    testHandlerStateSurvives [
        "The handler ran in THIS VM and left its marks here — which is what
         separates a door from a mock. WM_SIZE's lParam packs the new client
         size, low word width and high word height, so a handler that decoded
         it correctly has computed 400x600 out of one integer.

         Also the counter discipline the gate depends on: `messagesSeen`
         counts ARRIVALS (bumped before any handler can fail) and `sizeCount`
         counts SUCCESSES. Two different questions, and the raise tests in
         `just gate-wg2` need to tell them apart."
        | before |
        WinShell resetDoorCounters.
        before := WinShell messagesSeen.
        WinShell window: 0 message: (WinApi constant: 'WM_SIZE')
                 wParam: 0 lParam: 16r02580190.
        self assert: WinShell messagesSeen equals: before + 1.
        self assert: WinShell sizeCount equals: 1.
        self assert: WinShell lastMessage equals: (WinApi constant: 'WM_SIZE').
        self assert: (WinShell lastSizeSeen at: 1) equals: 400.
        self assert: (WinShell lastSizeSeen at: 2) equals: 600.
        "A second, different size overwrites — `lastSizeSeen` is the LATEST,
         which is what a coalescing layout in WG3 will read."
        WinShell window: 0 message: (WinApi constant: 'WM_SIZE')
                 wParam: 0 lParam: 16r012C00C8.
        self assert: (WinShell lastSizeSeen at: 1) equals: 200.
        self assert: (WinShell lastSizeSeen at: 2) equals: 300.
        self assert: WinShell sizeCount equals: 2.
        WinShell resetDoorCounters.
        self assert: WinShell sizeCount equals: 0.
        self assert: WinShell lastSizeSeen equals: nil
    ]

    testAllowlistMatchesWinkb [
        "The one place in this layer where Rust TRANSCRIBES Win32 numbers,
         checked against the database rather than trusted to care.

         `runtime::win_wndproc::ALLOWLIST` has to be a compile-time `const`
         (a `match` arm cannot query SQLite, and the core crate must build on
         a machine with no windows_api.db at all), so its six message numbers
         are written out in Rust — the exact thing D2 forbids everywhere
         else. This is the compensating control: the SAME six numbers, asked
         of winkb by name, must be the ones this file's dispatcher compares
         against. If a future sprint adds a seventh to the Rust list and gets
         the number wrong, the guest side will never match it and this test
         says so.

         The values are asserted literally as well, because 'both sides agree'
         and 'both sides are right' are different claims and only the second
         one keeps the window working."
        self assert: (WinApi constant: 'WM_CLOSE') equals: 16r0010.
        self assert: (WinApi constant: 'WM_DESTROY') equals: 16r0002.
        self assert: (WinApi constant: 'WM_SIZE') equals: 16r0005.
        self assert: (WinApi constant: 'WM_COMMAND') equals: 16r0111.
        self assert: (WinApi constant: 'WM_KEYDOWN') equals: 16r0100.
        self assert: (WinApi constant: 'WM_CHAR') equals: 16r0102.
        "WM_PAINT is deliberately NOT on the list — WG2 keeps
         DefWindowProcW's erase and painting is WG3/WG4's — so the number is
         pinned here to make its absence a decision rather than an oversight."
        self assert: (WinApi constant: 'WM_PAINT') equals: 16r000F.

        "loWord:/hiWord: are how every packed message is read, and getting
         either wrong produces a plausible number rather than an error."
        self assert: (WinApi loWord: 16rFFFF0001) equals: 1.
        self assert: (WinApi hiWord: 16rFFFF0001) equals: 65535.
        self assert: (WinApi loWord: 0) equals: 0.
        self assert: (WinApi hiWord: 0) equals: 0
    ]
]
